585:
(a) The place of operation of such machines, pool tables, miniature game tables, video games and similar amusements shall be (1) an eating establishment regularly serving prepared food where chairs, tables and counters are provided to seat 50 or more persons; or (2) a coin laundromat with ten or more washing machines, and where such establishments are located in zoning districts which permit such establishments and conforming uses.
(b) A permit must be obtained by all persons wishing to establish a location where such machines, pool tables, miniature game tables, video games and similar amusements are located. The application for the permit shall include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) The name and address of the owner-applicant;
(2) The address of the establishment;
(3) The number of such machines, pool tables, miniature game tables, video games and/or similar amusements;
(4) If the owner-applicant is a partnership, the names and residence addresses of all the partners;
(5) If the owner-applicant is a corporation, the names of the corporate officers;
(6) The name of the manager;
(7) The names of all shareholders holding more than ten percent of any class of corporate stock, or other entity having a financial interest in each entity which is to own or operate the licensed establishment.
(c) No license shall be granted to any person who has been convicted under any federal, state or local law of any misdemeanor involving moral turpitude within the last ten years prior to the filing of the application for such license.
(d) No license shall be granted to any person who has been convicted under any federal, state or local law of any felony within the last ten years prior to the filing of the application for such license.
(e) No license shall be granted to any person who has had any license previously issued by any jurisdiction revoked within the last two years prior to filing of the application for such license.
(f) All licenses granted shall be for a full calendar year period. License fees shall not be prorated and are nonrefundable.
(g) All licenses granted under this section shall expire on December 31 of each year.
(h) Licensees who desire to renew their licenses shall file application with all the applicable fees with the city clerk's office on the form provided before November 30 of each year.
(i) All licensed establishments must have and continuously maintain in the county a registered agent upon whom any process, notice or demand required or permitted by law or under this section to be served upon the licensee or owner may be served. This person must be a resident of the county. The licensee shall file the name of such agent, along with written consent of such agent with the city in the form as prescribed.
(j) The license fee for the operation of pinball machines, pool tables, miniature game tables, video games and similar amusements shall be $10.00 per machine, table or game per year.
(k) A license may be suspended or revoked by the city commission:
(1) When the licensee furnishes fraudulent or untruthful information in the application for a license or omits information required in the application for a license or for failure to pay all fees, taxes or other charges imposed under the provisions of this chapter;
(2) Where alcoholic beverages have been sold or distributed during a period of suspension;
(3) For good cause after hearing upon five days' written notice to the holder of such license of the time, place and purpose of the hearing and general statement of the charges to be considered. "Good cause" for the suspension or revocation of any such license shall consist of the violation of any laws or ordinances regulating such business; or violations of any alcoholic beverage regulations; or for the violation of any city ordinance, rule, regulation other than traffic ordinances; or for irregularities, omissions, or untruths in the application for a license;
(4) In order to protect the health, safety and welfare of the community. In making such a determination, the city commission shall assess the extent to which the granting of the license or the continued holding of the license would contribute to the following problems: littering in the area of the establishment, loitering in the area of the establishment, gambling in or around the area of the establishment, the public consumption of alcoholic beverages in the area of the establishment, the exposure of minors to the sale of alcoholic beverages without adult supervision.
(l) Sworn officers of the department of safety, the building inspector or codes enforcement officer or a designated representative of the above shall have authority to inspect establishments for the purpose of verifying compliance with the requirements of this section.
(m) The place of operation of pinball machines, pool tables, miniature game tables, video games and similar amusements shall not be located where an entrance to the establishment is located:
(1) Within 100 yards of the property line of a private residence, unless such residence is in a "C-2" zoning district;
(2) Within 100 yards of the property line of any school ground or college campus;
(3) Within 100 yards of the entrance to any MARTA rapid transit station; or
(4) Within 100 yards of the entrance to an existing establishment that is licensed for the purpose of providing pinball machines, pool tables, miniature game tables, video games and similar amusements.
(n) For purposes of this section, distances shall be measured by the most direct legal route of travel on the ground.
(Ord. No. O-95-09, 7-10-95)

646:
(a) Definitions.
(1) Camp shall mean residing in or using a public street, sidewalk or park for private living accommodations, such as erecting tents or other temporary structures or objects providing shelter; sleeping in a single place for any period of time exceeding 60 minutes; regularly cooking or preparing meals; or other similar activities.
(2) Storing personal property shall mean leaving one's personal effects, such as, but not limited to, clothing, bedrolls, cookware, sleeping bags, luggage, knapsacks or backpacks unattended for any period of time exceeding one hour. This term shall not include parking a bicycle or other mode of transportation.
(3) Public park includes all municipal parks, public playgrounds, public plazas, attractions and monuments.
(4) Public street includes all public streets and highways, public sidewalks, public benches, public parking lots and medians.
(b) Public parks. It shall be unlawful to camp or to store personal property in any park owned by the city.
(c) Public streets. It shall be unlawful to camp, to store personal property or to sit or to lie down on any public street as defined above.
(d) Other public property blocking ingress and egress. It shall be unlawful to camp, to store personal property or to sit or to lie down on any public property so as to interfere with ingress or egress from buildings.
(e) Warning. No person may be arrested for violation of this section until said person has received a written warning and has been given an opportunity to cease the unlawful conduct. If, after receiving such warning and opportunity, a person fails to comply with the warning, said person is subject to arrest for violation of this section.
(f) Exceptions. Notwithstanding anything in this section to the contrary, this section shall not apply to nor be construed to prohibit the following behavior:
(1) Persons sitting or lying down as a result of a medical emergency;
(2) Persons sitting in wheelchairs while using parks, sidewalks or other public places;
(3) Persons sitting down while attending parades for which valid permits have been issued;
(4) Persons sitting down while patronizing outdoor cafes;
(5) Persons sitting down, lying down or napping while attending performances, festivals and concerts taking place in any park, street or sidewalk;
(6) Persons sitting on chairs or benches supplied by a public agency or abutting private property owner;
(7) Persons sitting on seats in bus zones occupied by people waiting for the bus;
(8) Persons sitting or lying down while waiting in an orderly line outside a box office to purchase tickets to any sporting event, concert, performance, other special event;
(9) Persons sitting or lying down while waiting in an orderly line awaiting entry to any building, including shelters, or awaiting social services, such as provision of meals; or
(10) Children under the age of 13 years sleeping in parks, or sleeping anywhere else while being carried by an accompanying person or while sitting or lying in a stroller or baby carriage.
(11) Persons operating mobile food facilities, where allowed as a limited use and where such activities do not conflict with standards set forth the by the city.
(Ord. No. O-97-20, 6-16-97; Ord. No. O-17-04, § 1.3, 5-15-17; Ord. No. O-22-11, § 2(Exh. A), 10-17-22)

675:
